From 76c85f1ef46dbeccde9b21b806b9769df7e33398 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris McLaughlin <19711592+qBitnaut@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:54:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] ipa: simple: awb: leave clipped pixels out of the grey
 world sums

The software ISP AWB is a plain grey world: the gains are the ratios of
the sums of the channels over the statistics window, clipped pixels
included. In bright scenes (outdoors, windows, sky) a fifth or more of
the green samples clip while red and blue, which have a lower response,
mostly do not. The sums then underestimate the red and blue gains and
the picture has a green cast.

Make SwStatsCpu also sum the channels over the 2x2 blocks in which no
sample reaches 97% of the full scale, and count the blocks sampled and
the blocks left unclipped. Both the CPU and the EGL debayer gather their
statistics with SwStatsCpu, so both get the new fields.

The AWB algorithm uses the sums of the unclipped blocks, with the black
level removed per unclipped block, once at least 15% of the blocks are
unclipped, and the sums of all blocks below 5%, blending the two gains
in between so that they do not jump. The gains also move by half of the
distance to the new estimate per update after the first, to keep them
from hunting when the clipped blocks follow the exposure.

Signed-off-by: Chris McLaughlin <19711592+qBitnaut@users.noreply.github.com>
